      "snippet": {
        "text": "This paper is about research conducted in middle and high schools. It was designed considering the need to enhance the AI literacy of K-12 students. Focusing on that, researchers decided to test the extent to which CyberScholar has a potential to support K-12 students' writing in diverse subject areas. The investigation was conducted, particularly given that the capabilities of the tool, provide students with the opportunity to have AI feedback that directly connects to their teachers' work and expectations, thus enabling them to revise their writing, based on the AI review, initially guided and customized by their teachers. This ensures that students experience an AI review process based on reliable source materials connected to the interests and demands of the curriculum. \n\nIt was thus taken into account how AI can be harnessed to facilitate opportunities to improve equity in education by providing feedback and learning experiences to diverse populations in an inclusive and efficient manner. CyberScholar, as a scalable web platform, is designed to address resource disparities between school districts by meeting the personalized needs of students from diverse backgrounds across several factors: identity, cultural capital, and access to resources. \n\nTo have diverse populations involved, the flyer created to invite teachers to participate with their students was distributed across different states in the US. Despite this intention, the selection criteria were not purposive. Instead, convenience sampling was adopted, i.e., a non-probability sampling technique was used in which participants are selected based on their accessibility and proximity to the researcher, rather than through random selection. This was employed due to constraints related to time, resources, and logistics that limited the feasibility of the investigation. However, when teachers expressed their interest, the research team made sure to check whether diverse populations were included. While convenience sampling may be understood to compromise the representativeness of the sample and introduce potential bias, it remains a practical and widely accepted strategy in exploratory research, and qualitative inquiries, such as this one. By prioritizing ease of recruitment, researchers could efficiently gather preliminary insights, even if generalizability to the broader population is limited.",
