      "snippet": {
        "text": "This research was conducted in several phases. Initially, two intact classes were randomly selected to form the control and experimental groups. To ensure fairness, a writing examination was administered as a pretest after confirming that all participants were similar in their mother language, age, and English proficiency level. Before the treatment, Fig. 3 Procedural overview: Activities conducted by each group on a weekly basis participants from both groups took a pretest to assess their reading proficiency, as well as completed questionnaires regarding their engagement and self-regulation. To eliminate potential biases related to teachers, the same instructor was chosen to teach both the experimental and control groups. The duration of the experiment was approximately 12 weeks. \n\nParticipants from both groups were expected to attend in-person sessions twice a week, with each session lasting 90 min. In this study, we implemented a reading instructional mode that encompassed a blend of teacher-led instruction, collaborative activities, and independent reading. Both the experimental and control groups were exposed to instruction grounded in this mode, differing primarily in the integration of a distinct intervention exclusively within the treatment group. In the comparison group homework tasks were assigned to the participants, and they received feedback from either the instructor or their classmates. In contrast, the experimental group used AI-powered applications to improve their reading comprehension as part of their homework assignments. The instructor was responsible for configuring the RTM platform and ensuring that the participants' tablets or smartphones were ready before the classroom commenced. She subsequently provided an explanation of the app's functionalities and features, entailing instructions on how to accomplish activities, obtain feedback, and utilize the upgrade system effectively. Subsequently, the instructor assigned homework to the participants, directing them to accomplish the activities using the RTM platform. \n\nAfter the intervention, the instructor employed the same assessment methodology and test tool to administer a posttest for both the treatment and comparison groups. \n\nThe posttest was administered one week after the intervention period ended, facilitating comparative analysis of the group's performance after the instructional intervention. The students were instructed to complete a posttest assessing their reading proficiency, along with a questionnaire that examined aspects of self-regulation and engagement. To enhance the depth of the study, the qualitative phase was included which involved semistructured interviews conducted after the analysis of the posttest scores.",
