        "text": "Writing proficiency is essential for the active engagement of young individuals in civic life and the global economy (Graham & Hebert, 2010). Nevertheless, most students in the U.S. have not achieved the required proficiency levels in writing according to grade standards (Passonneau et al., 2017). According to the National Assessment of Educational Progress [NAEP] report on writing assessment in 2017, only 28% of fourth graders, along with 27% of the eighth and twelfth graders, perform at or above the proficient level. Addressing this crisis in writing proficiency across elementary, middle, and high schools demands the collective attention and efforts of all K-12 educational stakeholders to enhance writing instruction and outcomes. \n\nHowever, enhancing K-12 students' writing proficiency poses great challenges, stemming from issues faced at the classroom level by teachers and at the policy level. First, current K-12 English language arts (ELA) curricula often prioritize reading and other subjects over writing (Graham, 2019), resulting in inadequate time devoted to writing instruction. Second, teachers report a lack of preparation in writing instruction (Graham et al., 2014), which may hinder their ability to implement evidence-based practices (EBPs), or practices supported by clear, consistent, and convincing scientific research (Troia, 2014). Consequently, researchers and educators strive to explore effective interventions/practice to assist teachers in overcoming time constraints and inadequate preparation for adopting EBPs, and thereby enhance students' writing skills. \n\nA promising approach to assist K-12 teachers in addressing these challenges involves using technology in writing classrooms (Graham et al., 2012). Researchers have examined the potential of technology-based approaches to assist educators in addressing challenges within writing classrooms and have assessed their impact on enhancing students' writing abilities (Applebee & Langer, 2009;Goldberg et al., 2003;Little et al., 2018). Encouragingly, the use of technology has demonstrated a generally favorable effect on students' writing progress within the U.S. K-12 educational setting (Goldberg et al., 2003;Graham & Perin, 2007;Little et al., 2018). \n\nAs artificial intelligence (AI) continues to advance rapidly, it transforms into an advancing class of foundational capabilities that are increasingly integrated into various educational technology systems (U.S.",
