      "snippet": {
        "text": "As artificial intelligence (AI) continues to advance rapidly, it transforms into an advancing class of foundational capabilities that are increasingly integrated into various educational technology systems (U.S. Department of Education, Office of Educational Technology, 2023). AI-empowered technological tools offer innovative solutions to augment traditional writing instruction and provide personalized support to students on a large scale (Rad et al., 2023). By harnessing the capabilities of AI, teachers in writing classrooms can implement tailored instruction to address the diverse needs of students, thereby mitigating challenges associated with writing instruction and improving writing outcomes. One state-ofthe-art technology-based tool that is empowered by sophisticated AI techniques is automated writing evaluation (AWE), which is intended for use as a formative assessment tool and facilitates multiple EBPs in writing instruction (Palermo & Thomson, 2018;Warschauer & Grimes, 2008). AWE refers to computer systems that utilize AI and natural language processing (NLP) to evaluate student essays, providing holistic scores, diagnostic feedback, and suggestions for improvement during revisions (Wilson et al., 2021b). AWE, as an AI-enhanced formative assessment tool, has the potential to benefit writing instruction, thereby contributing to students' improvement in writing by (a) saving teachers' time spent on grading and granting them more time for instruction and assisting students (U.S. Department of Education, Office of Educational Technology, 2023; Wilson et al., 2021a), (b) providing a supportive writing environment (see Wilson & Roscoe, 2020), (c) facilitating formative assessment through enhancing the feedback loops that contribute to instructional improvement, providing timely, frequent and personalized feedback (see Graham et al., 2015;Liaqat et al., 2021), (d) aiding teachers in delivering higher-level feedback (see Schroeder et al., 2008), and (e) encouraging revision (see Wilson & Andrada, 2016). \n\nThe growing popularity of AWE in K-12 settings has attracted more districts and schools to adopt or consider adopting it. However, the rapid implementation has surpassed research efforts (Shermis et al., 2016), particularly in evaluating AWE efficacy within the U.S. K-12 context.",
