      "snippet": {
        "text": "One use of AI in language education is to deliver tailored and adaptive instruction to match individual students' needs (Huang et al., 2023). From Vygotsky's zone of proximal development (Vygotsky, 1978), it is understood that students learn best if they are presented with tasks that they are ready for, given that they are assisted in their learning with the correct support and scaffolding (Ferguson et al., 2022;Song & Song, 2023). For example, the popular automated writing evaluation software Grammarly provides EFL learners specific suggestions to improve their own written texts (Dizon & Gayed, 2021;Kloppers, 2023). AI-based tools such as grammar checkers and automatic writing assessment can also assist teachers in promptly identifying problems and providing feedback and grading to students (Bateson, 2021;Dizon & Gayed, 2021;Lu, 2019;Mizumoto & Eguchi, 2023). While teachers can also achieve these purposes without the aid of AI, its use can help achieve better assessment accuracy and can help reduce the load on teachers' time that can be used for other areas of their instruction (Baidoo-Anu & Owusu Ansah, 2023;Lu, 2019). \n\nFurthermore, with the use of AI, more engaging learning experiences can be created, particularly because of the interactive options that AI can provide (Bang et al., 2020;Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019). AI-powered chatbots can provide human-like interactions to assist learners in personalized ways (Hsu et al., 2021;Jeon, 2022). It is recognized that this helps overcome the problem that, normally, students only have few opportunities to practice English in actual conversations (Kl ımov a & Ibna Seraj, 2023). This way, AI-powered chatbots and virtual language trainers can allow students to practice their language skills in a more immersive and authentic way than earlier CALL tools, helping improve motivation and reduce learning anxiety (Barcomb & Cardoso, 2020;Kohnke et al., 2023).",
