      "snippet": {
        "text": "1 Introduction AI, in general, is an artificial intelligence that assists in jobs in most every field of work. Generative AI (GAI) assists in the field of communication by pulling information stored in LLMs or large language models. A few well-known GAIs are ChatGPT, Google's Gemini chatbot, and Microsoft's Copilot. It works when a person asks it a question or gives it a prompt. Generative AI then produces a document fitting the criteria of the prompt or answering the posed question. Due to the quick discourse results, GAI, such as ChatGPT, can be useful in a composition course. In addition to composing a document within seconds, students may use generative AI to improve their writing skills, grammar, and articulation by applying the suggestions provided by the generative AI. However, generative AI also has potential pitfalls. Students may abuse it for short cuts and cheating, which can inhibit learning and make it difficult for teachers to measure course outcomes. GAI can also encourage other negative behavior and habits that hinder students and their potential success. \n\nAs generative AI becomes more mainstream, it is utilized more often in the classroom. Some students use it for assistance, some use it to cheat, and some do not use it at all, mostly because they do not know how to. . .yet, but it is just a matter of time before they also will use generative AI. According to a new HEPI Policy note, 53% of students use generative AI to help them with assessments [3]. This emerging situation demands a sound policy that provides guidance on how to use generative AI ethically: as a learning tool to enhance creative, well-developed writing and critical thinking and not as a means for cheating that inhibits skill and character growth. When designed correctly, a generative AI policy is a perfect opportunity to not only improve writing skills but also cultivate virtues and build character within student users because ethical GAI usage requires honesty and self-control. \n\nAccording to Vallor [4], the ancient philosophical concept of virtue is that it is \"any stable trait\" (p. 17) that excel [s] in fulfilling its distinctive function.",
