        "text": "It is helpful to understand the effectiveness of AI by further dissecting the factors that could affect its effectiveness. This can be divided into intervention factors and outcome measures. The intervention factors of interest in this scoping review are: \n\n(1) the language of intervention (i.e., L1 or non-L1) \n\n(2) AI type (i.e., narrow or general AI) \n\n(3) AI factors (i.e., features specific to the deployment of the AI tool). \n\nAI factors were gathered from descriptions of the AI intervention. Outcome measures were also of concern as these also differ across the studies and may affect the impact of AI. Figures 4, 5, and 6 display the effectiveness of AI as characterized by these intervention factors and outcome measures. The language of intervention, AI type and outcome measures included looking into studies that had comparisons between AI and non-AI groups. Meanwhile, By language of intervention, AI is effective for both L1 and non-L1 languages. For standardization, non-L1 was defined as languages aside from L1, as the studies explored in this scoping review had differing specificities in their description of languages other than L1 (e.g., foreign language learners, second language, or English language learners for children who had other languages as their L1). Of the five articles with non-L1 interventions, three explicitly listed the language of intervention as children's L2. When statistical analyses were conducted, almost all articles for both L1 (n = 2) and non-L1 (n = 5) achieved either significant findings where AI outperformed traditional methods, or nonsignificant findings in which AI performed equally well as conventional teaching strategies. The nonsignificant results were found for Mandarin Chinese L1 where the children who children who had a robot reading companion and those who had a human reading companion fared similarly on a reading comprehension test (Yueh et al., 2020). One article involving Italian L1 did not conduct statistical analyses, but descriptive results also supported the notion that the AI intervention using a motion-based environment to practice phonological awareness was effective (Goffredo et al., 2016). All articles on non-L1 interventions found that the AI group was more effective.",
