        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Such categorization of AI into narrow and general may be useful for dissecting how the technological capabilities of AI can affect its effectiveness when deployed in language education settings. \n\nNarrow AI has been employed to promote children's language skills to some success, being linked to better language and literacy learning outcomes such as in storytelling, vocabulary, literacy knowledge, reading, handwriting acquisition and more (Bonneton-Botté et al., 2020;Huang & Wang, 2023;Leddo et al., 2019;Lee et al., 2023;Zhang et al., 2022). Computer-assisted software programs that incorporate AI and AI electronic book reading programs have retained popularity in language interventions since their inception (Macaruso & Rodman, 2011). More recently, research has studied the use of social and humanoid robots. These robots are well regarded as educational agents that can effectively deliver educational content to children through offering unique learning experiences for children (Chang et al., 2010;Woods et al., 2004). Some social robots can be classified as general AI due to their functions across multiple domains, such as possessing features for emotional recognition on top of providing personalized practice lessons. They function as well as human tutors in providing educational resources, with the potential to even outperform traditional teaching methods (de Wit et al., 2018). To this effect, most robot AI applications that have been utilized in previous research have been designed to take on the role of a child's tutor, learning companion or caregiver (Tanaka & Matsuzoe, 2012;Kory-Westlund et al., 2017). \n\nAs more open-source AI formats spur on development in related technologies and tools for AI in language and literacy education are met with general acceptance and positive attitudes by both parents (Lin et al., 2021) and teachers (Yim & Wegerif, 2024), there is an increased need to truly evaluate the effectiveness of using AI in early language education. This allows stakeholders in children's language education to identify whether current AI tools adopted can solve current gaps in the language environment and inform potential investment in these tools.",
