        "text": "Given the increasing use of GenAI in educational contexts, particularly in academic writing, it has become essential to investigate and consider students' GenAI literacy when exploring AI-assisted writing practices. While general AI literacy has been defined and explored extensively in previous frameworks (Long and Magerko, 2020;Ng et al., 2021), the unique characteristics of generative technologies necessitate a more specialised literacy framework. Researchers have highlighted that GenAI literacy involves not only understanding how generative tools function, but also mastering practical skills to effectively prompt these tools, critically evaluate AI-generated content, and navigate ethical and contextual complexities specific to GenAI-generated outputs (Zhao, Cox and Cai, 2024;Bozkurt, 2024). Despite recognition of the importance of GenAI literacy for meaningful interaction with tools like ChatGPT and Gemini, there remains limited empirical research that explicitly examines how students' varying levels of GenAI literacy impact their academic writing performances when GenAI assistance is provided or withdrawn (Annapureddy, Fornaroli and Gatica-Perez, 2024;Lyu, Wang, Chung, Sun and Zhang, 2024). Addressing this gap is imperative, as understanding the relationship between students' GenAI literacy and their academic writing outcomes can inform practical strategies for educators seeking to effectively integrate these advanced technologies into multimodal academic writing instruction (Chiu, 2024;Annapureddy et al., 2024).",
