      "snippet": {
        "text": "Advances in Artificial Intelligence (AI) represent a potential paradigmatic shift for evaluation practice and evaluation education. AI and machine learning (ML) are fields that focus on creating computer programs and systems that can learn to perform tasks usually done by humans (Norvig & Russell, 2010). AI is the broader field, while ML is a subset of AI techniques that mainly use data to make predictions or decisions (Thornton, 2023). Generative AI is a class of AI techniques that use existing data to attempt to learn and mimic patterns in the data to create novel synthesized data based on the training data. OpenAI's ChatGPT is an example of a generative AI tool developed from the generative pre-trained transformer (GPT) series of large language models (LLMs) that have been trained to perform natural language processing (NLP) tasks such as processing, decoding, and generating natural language (Merrit, 2023). The result is a web-based question and answer chat interface that users can have conversations with. \n\nAI and generative AI offer possibilities for improving evaluation education. This article begins with a brief introduction to the intersection of AI and evaluator education. We follow with three specific examples of challenges in evaluator education that could be purposefully addressed with AI tools, and our processes and reflections about prototyping AI tools with the purpose of improving evaluator education. We conclude with next steps for generative AI in evaluation education, grounded in the position that both educators and students should develop AI literacy to use these new tools appropriately and actively mitigate the risks of misuse.",
