      "snippet": {
        "text": "In the domain of chatbots for education, a number of articles and approaches are available. Machine learning for developing conversational agents has been a unique area of focus for about the past two decades. Farhan et al. in 2012, have presented a study that describes a web-based chatbot named Pandora. It is an e-learning platform that aims to address the issue of delayed responses to student inquiries. The chatbot predicts future events based on internet keywords. It utilizes AIML-stored questions and answers, with human intervention required when the chatbot cannot provide a suitable response. \n\nNiranjan et al., in 2012, proposed a chatbot designed to enhance the efficiency of question answering processes. They have described interdependent components within the architecture of the chatbot. At the forefront lies the interface engineered to emulate real-time interactions between students and the educational system. The tokenizer functions as a linguistic disassembler tasked with segmenting the student's inquiry into discernible tokens. The stop words eliminator winnows out extraneous linguistic elements (stop words) from the query corpus to reduce search complexity. Central to the system's functionality is the Bayesian Module which uses the Naïve Bayesian algorithm. It narrows down potential answers until the most pertinent response is discerned. The paper also discusses three foundational databases. The category database is composed of various knowledge categories and the most common words associated with each category. The question database stores a repository of questions categorized under each knowledge domain. The answer database houses responses to queries cataloged within the question database. Then the Java speech engine-powered text-to-speech converter is encountered. A human agent referred to as the tutor assumes an administrative mantle, overseeing database management and curating question-answer repositories. \n\nColace et al., have suggested a comprehensive AI-based conversation model comprising four principal modules: the front-end module, the back-office module, the knowledge base module, and the e-learning BOT Module. The front-end module serves as the user-facing interface, facilitating seamless interaction across a myriad of devices including tablets, smartphones, and PCs, thereby ensuring widespread accessibility and user engagement (Colace et al. 2018).",
