      "snippet": {
        "text": "Cipherbot is an AI chatbot facilitating students' interaction with learning materials by integrating artificial intelligence (AI) and cloud-based technologies. Cipherbot allows educators to upload learning materials. Students can then inquire about any courserelated topic and receive contextualized responses based on the provided learning materials (see Figure 1). Cipherbot exemplifies the rise of AI in education [11] and the incorporation of Generative AI (GenAI) into learning and teaching practices [11,13,23,23]. GenAI and its subtechnologies, such as large language models (LLMs), are rapidly transforming how students learn, shifting the focus from documents to dialogue. This means that instead of reading a textbook, students now engage with AI chatbots to learn new concepts, engage in knowledge integration, and develop their understanding of a given topic [25]. Historically, education has predominantly relied on static documents such as textbooks, assignment sheets, presentations, and videos. The transition to a dialogue-based approach, spearheaded by educational AI chatbots enabled by GenAI and LLMs, remains understudied due to its novelty. As the educational sector continues adopting AI-based tools, techno-educational research must provide empirical evidence on patterns observed when students use these tools in real classroom settings. \n\nIn the big picture, the reader might ask, \"Why are AI chatbots needed in education? What problems do they exactly solve?\". While the answers to these questions can be multifaceted, the main reasoning is that AI chatbots offer personalized support to learners [28]. This personalized support is seen valuable because learners vary by their attributes such as prior knowledge, intellect, comprehension, and so, meaning that some students require more support to understand concepts than others [4,20,36]. Also, students also have varying interests, so their questions and specific learning objectives might vary, for which personalized support is required. Human educators are often unable to provide individual-level personalized support due to resource limitations, which has driven interest toward technology, specifically educational chatbots, as personalized support agents [26]. \n\nHowever, the educational community lacks guiding principles on effectively and responsibly leveraging these AI technologies. First, to provide effective learning, educational AI chatbots need to be usable [6].",
