        "text": "AI for educational support has evolved from early intelligent tutoring systems that delivered finely scripted, stepwise feedback to contemporary generative systems that promise broad, cross-subject support [6,26,55,70,108,137]. Early ITS work demonstrated that computer-based tutors can diagnose errors and scaffold problem solving at scale [103,130,131,142], while also revealing the costs of authoring and domain specificity [5,60,88,90]. Recent GenAI efforts consolidate some typical use cases, such as one-to-one tutoring and feedback for students [21,41,85], automated content and hint generation [110,120,138], and teacher-facing assistance for planning and assessment [15,134,137]. \n\nMathematics has been an early target for GenAI tutoring and feedback as a primary testbed [9,85,110,144]. Recent studies have evaluated LLMs' stepwise help and hint generation, with a primary focus on solution correctness or offline benchmarks with simulated learners [46,83,138,147]. However, solving well does not necessarily mean tutoring well [9,46]. Models that can derive correct answers may still struggle to pace support, elicit, and build on student thinking, diagnose misconceptions, and calibrate the granularity of hints to sustain productive struggle [50,59,85,116,122]. In parallel, LLMs have been used to analyze classroom discourse, for example, classifying tutor or student \"talk moves\" to inform instruction, yet these analytics systems support teachers rather than act as student-facing collaborators [76,89,139,148]. \n\nIn the K-12 education context, early adolescents (e.g., middle school students) are still developing self-regulation and are highly sensitive to peer dynamics, which shapes how help, authority, and timing are received [10,20,42,124]. Mathematical pedagogy emphasizes productive struggle and teacher orchestration of discussion [132], implying that AI must calibrate granularity, tone, and intervention timing to classroom norms [51,59,122].",
