        "text": "Feedback is one of the most powerful methods to enhance learning [38,115]. However, providing targeted feedback to students' open-ended written work requires substantial efforts from experts [15,40,60,88]. Recent research has shown mixed results on using large language models (LLMs) to generate feedback in educational contexts. Some studies have shown promise-AI feedback can exhibit more desirable properties as judged by teachers or automated evaluators [16,22]. Only a handful of studies show improvements on student outcomes. For example, [75] found in a randomized trial that receiving AI-generated feedback increased middle school students' revision performance and task motivation compared to receiving no feedback. [53] found that feedback generated by Chat-GPT was more effective than pre-written static feedback in improving student's writing performance. [24] showed that students rated AI-generated content to be of similar quality to peer-generated content. On the other hand, research has revealed frequent LLM mistakes. For example, 50% of math-tutoring dialogues contain mistakes [36]; AI-generated next-step actions reach only 52%-70% accuracy in TutorGym [114]; and 30% of ChatGPT algebra hints were rejected for low quality [86]. These errors in AI-generated feedback are found to negatively impact student learning outcomes [7]. \n\nTo address AI limitations in producing pedagogical materials, one line of promising research is to explore human-AI collaboration. For example, Tutor Copilot [111] shows exciting results that when human tutors receive AI suggestions during tutoring sessions, it leads to higher learning gains. Similarly, two studies this year [37,106] have found that human-AI tutoring outperforms AI tutoring alone in middle school math. However, there has been limited exploration on human-AI complementary feedback provision. \n\nWe highlight two gaps in prior research that this project aims to address. (1) First, we explore AI-mediated feedback, where human evaluators receive AI suggestions during feedback provision. In particular, we situate our work in knowledge-intensive essays where students need to demonstrate accurate conceptual understanding and analyses of phenomena through writing.",
