      "snippet": {
        "text": "The use of OpenAI's conversational AI model, commonly known as ChatGPT, has become increasingly popular in the last two years, providing a new perspective on educational technology. Essentially, Generative Pretrained Transformer (GPT) is an AI-powered model developed by OpenAI that generates human-like text based on input data. The interface of ChatGPT is a chatbot launched on November 30, 2022. A chatbot is a software program that enables interaction between the user and the complex set of algorithms that makes up an AI's cognitive network (Wu et al., 2023). In simpler terms, the chatbot is the interface that allows users to dialogue with ChatGPT. This AI tool uses Large Language Models (LLM), which is a class of machine-learning models for natural language processing and can be interacted with using natural language (De Kok, 2023;Meyer et al., 2023). LLMs not only analyze vast amounts of textual data and infer relationships between words within a text but also enhance their capabilities alongside the size increase of datasets and inputs. The application possibilities for this technology range from analyzing cases about various sectors -from productive sectors to sociology -and from generating complex texts, even at an academic level, to writing entire sections of coding strings in various programming languages (Aljanabi, Ghazi, Hussein & Abas Abed, 2023). Currently, major concerns associated with implementing this new technology into the education sector revolve around ethical implications tied to academic integrity and plagiarism (Barrett & Pack, 2023). Indeed, AI-generated texts are now indistinguishable from human-produced ones and the most frequent observations emerging from the current literature fluctuate between enthusiasm and concern (Chen, Chen & Lin, 2020;Lim et al., 2023;Osamor, Kulume & Cherukut, 2023;Xiong, Shi, Pu & Liu, 2023). Nevertheless, education institutions have gone through similar pathways of acceptance in the past, such as 