        "text": "Models can generate comprehension and expansion questions, provide summaries, provide explanations and definitions of unknown words, and adjust responses to suit learners' proficiency levels [97]. \n\nThe study by Xiao et al. [98] implemented a reading comprehension exercise generation system aimed at providing high-quality and personalized reading materials for middle-school English learners in China. The system used LLMs to generate reading passages and corresponding questions. Extensive evaluations, both automatic and manual, demonstrated that the system-generated materials were suitable for students and, in some cases, surpassed the quality of existing human-written content. \n\nSäuberli and Clematide [99] explored how LLMs can be employed to generate and evaluate multiple-choice reading comprehension items. The study addressed the challenges of manual test creation and quality assurance by leveraging Llama 2 and GPT-4 to automate the process. The findings suggest that LLMs are capable of generating items of acceptable quality in a zero-shot setting. It was shown usage of LLMs is a promising approach for generating and evaluating reading comprehension test items, in particular for languages without large amount of data. \n\nNevertheless, concerns remain regarding the factual accuracy of generated responses, limited personalization, biases in the training data, potential plagiarism, and other ethical issues [98,[100][101][102]. As LLM technology evolves, many of these problems will be addressed, leading to an increased influence of LLMs in the educational sector. For instance, a workshop Generative AI for Education (GAIED) at NeurIPS2 is dedicated to the usage of LLMs for educational purposes, reflecting a commitment to address these concerns [103].",
