        "text": "The previous studies related to the topic of this research varied between these, scientific research, and books. The research team focused on recent studies for presentation and benefit, highlighting similarities and differences with the current research as follows: Bai et al. (2025) investigated young children's acceptance of AI-generated pedagogical agents (AIPAs) in picture book videos, focusing on the effects of voice and appearance. Using a 2 × 2 experimental design, the study found no significant differences in reading performance or cognitive load between AIPAs and real teachers. Children showed comparable preferences, supporting the potential of AIPAs as effective, low-load educational tools in early childhood learning Istiq'faroh et al. (2025) explored the use of AI-based children's storybooks to enhance reading interest and support elementary education in Indonesia. Using the ADDIE model and Fry readability, the study found that AI tools significantly improved student engagement and reading levels. The books aligned well with students' cognitive development and received high expert validation, highlighting their potential to reduce educational disparities and support effective teaching. Tan et al. (2025) explored how generative AI tools can assist students in constructing a consistent voice in multimodal writing. The study focused on a photo essay assignment where students used AI to generate images for their stories. Findings revealed two main practices, layering and blending, through which students shaped their unique voices. The research emphasizes the potential of AI tools in enhancing multimodal writing but also highlights challenges related to students' prompt literacy and AI limitations. \n\nvon Gillern (2025) explored how preservice teachers used AI to create children's literature as part of a literacy methods course. The study found that participants engaged in diverse creative processes, valued the outcomes, and experienced both excitement and challenges. The research highlights the potential of integrating AI tools into teacher education to foster creativity and innovation in literacy instruction. \n\nChen (2025) examined the use of AI in early childhood education, focusing on its developmental appropriateness and ethical considerations. The study discusses the opportunities and challenges AI presents for educators and proposes strategies for integrating AI tools in a way that supports equitable learning and fosters responsible engagement among young children.",
