      "snippet": {
        "text": "Despite these advances, studies have also revealed that many digital tools still lack personalization and adaptability, offering a one-size-fits-all approach that fails to meet the diverse needs and learning styles of individual students (McNamara et al., 2006;Serrano-Mendizábal et al., 2023). Moreover, real-time feedback and assessment capabilities are often limited, hindering the identification and remediation of reading difficulties (Kuhn et al., 2022). This highlights the need for exploring innovative instructional methods, such as the application of AI, to further enhance reading instruction (Shafiee Rad et al., 2024a). \n\nAI, which replicates human cognitive processes through machines, especially computer systems, presents exciting opportunities for advancing language learning (Dodigovic, 2007). AI-driven technologies, such as chatbots, speech recognition tools, language learning applications, and virtual tutors, have revolutionized education by offering learners dynamic and immersive experiences (Zou et al., 2023). With capabilities like real-time feedback, adaptive assessments, and tailored content, these technologies hold significant promise for enhancing learners' communication skills and expediting language acquisition (Shafiee Rad et al., 2024a). Moreover, AI promotes learner autonomy, engagement, and self-regulation by enabling students to independently access resources and receive feedback at their own convenience (Hsu et al., 2023). By offering personalized learning pathways tailored to learners' progress, preferences, and learning styles, AI effectively tackles the varied challenges faced by English language learners, such as restricted access to native speakers, delayed feedback, and limited individualized support (Wang et al., 2023). \n\nAlthough research specifically exploring the impact of AI on reading and comprehension skills among L2 learners remains scarce, studies focusing on other language skills offer compelling evidence of its effectiveness (Popenici & Kerr, 2017;Shafiee Rad et al., 2024a;Wang et al., 2023). However, these studies often focus on college learners, involve small sample sizes, and lack sufficient exploration of various language learning skills.",
