        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Numerous research results show that developers have updated intelligent chatbot technology to provide real-time feedback and create immersive user learning experiences. These functions can be an excellent way to alleviate the pressure on learners when learning spoken language. Researchers develop personalized learning pathways for users. Compared to traditional English classrooms, mobile learning environments can enhance learning motivation. Therefore, this study assesses the interactions between intrinsic and extrinsic motivational factors and learning outcomes when using AI chatbots. \n\nMost successful learners use various speaking strategies to enhance their English-speaking performance. Researchers identified extensive practice as critical to improving spoken English proficiency [17]. indicated that the AI chatbot substantially encouraged student participation in conversation [18]. Research by Yang, Kim, Lee, and Shin [18] indicated that participants' survey responses not only highlighted the positive potential of chatbots in EFL settings. The study demonstrated that the AI Chatbot's contextual learning environment significantly improved learners' English speaking and listening abilities. Integrating AI chatbots into educational systems significantly impacted academic progress, mainly by impacting teachers and students. While these technologies offered personalized instruction and automated assessments, they also presented an ethical and practical challenge [19], [20]. While countries like Malaysia actively adopted AI chatbots as part of the Fourth Industrial Revolution, most higher-education institutions were still not fully prepared to adopt AI-based chatbots as a virtual student services support solution, indicating significant regional disparities [21]. These findings indicate that students from different regions exhibited varying levels of technological proficiency in adapting to and using AI chatbots [22]. Research on the application of educational chatbots, particularly among Chinese English-speaking learners, is limited and requires further exploration. \n\nThe results show that it is difficult for people to perceive text from the dimension of social intelligence. They enjoy the pleasant experience of human-like behavior when interacting with natural language. Nevertheless, it feels terrible and unadaptable when the autonomous agent happens incorrectly or at the wrong time to express behavior. [23]. Therefore, ''human-likeness,'' the degree to which an AI system resembles a human, has become a crucial consideration in design. The findings demonstrate that chatbots can assist learning in a tutor-like environment while exploring additional avenues and techniques for assessing their quality. [24].",
