      "snippet": {
        "text": "We begin Learning Disabilities Research & Practice (Volume 34), with two timely topics--free and appropriate education (FAPE) for students with learning disabilities (LD), and the development of culturally and linguistically responsive Individualized Education Programs (IEPs). Both issues are mandated by law; as noted by authors, however, legislative mandates are often not met, resulting in the erosion of appropriate instruction for all students with LD. Three research articles are then presented-two multiple-baseline studies (instruction for sentence writing fluency and video modeling instruction for mathematics), and a review of experimental studies targeting fraction learning. \n\nIn The Erosion of FAPE for Students with Learning Disabilities, Calhoon, Berkeley, and Scanlon \"call\" the field to \"arms\" in light of the U.S. Supreme Court's 2017 Endrew F. v. Douglas County School District decision that mandates education with meaningful benefit for students with disabilities. In the context of current practices, the authors call special educators, teacher educators, researchers, and policy makers to examine (1) placement, and the appropriateness of inclusive classrooms to support individually appropriate instruction; and (2) the quality of special education preparation programs for preparing specialists to address the learning needs of students with LD. \n\nIn a qualitative study of 30 IEPs written for English Learners with LD, Hoover, Erickson, Patton, Sacco, and Tran examine the inclusion of culturally and linguistically diverse qualities, strengths, and needs, as required in federal mandates. As reported in Examining IEPs of English Learners with Learning Disabilities for Cultural and Linguistic Responsiveness, little attention was given to culture and language in the IEPs' present levels of academic achievement and functional performance, measurable annual goals, special factors/delivery, and accommodations. The authors conclude their research by providing recommendations, tools, and eight principles for guiding the development of culturally and linguistically responsive IEPs for English Learners with LD. \n\nIn Text Writing within Simple Sentences: A Writing Fluency Intervention for Students with High-Incidence Disabilities, Datchuk and Rodgers examine the effects of a multicomponent intervention plus practice for improving a critical foundational skill: students' simple sentence writing accuracy and fluency.",
